        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Passage 3 — meta 12 min (mais difícil)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Does Multitasking Work?",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Many people claim they can work effectively while checking messages, listening to podcasts, or switching between tasks. Research, however, suggests that what we call multitasking is usually rapid switching, and each switch has a cost."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In a well-known study at Stanford University, participants who described themselves as heavy multitaskers performed worse on tests of attention than those who rarely multitasked. Surprisingly, they were also worse at switching between tasks — the very skill they believed they had developed."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Not all researchers interpret these findings in the same way. Some suggest that people who multitask a lot may already have weaker attention control, rather than multitasking causing the problem. Distinguishing cause from correlation, they argue, requires long-term studies that have not yet been carried out."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Nevertheless, there is broad agreement that some combinations are less harmful than others. Activities that use different resources — such as walking while listening to music — interfere relatively little. Tasks that require the same resource, like reading an email while listening to a lecture, compete directly for attention."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The practical lesson, the writer believes, is not to abandon multitasking entirely but to be selective: protect focused time for demanding work, and save routine tasks for moments when attention is less critical."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
